    "answer": "Query: \"What drug-response opportunities does olaparib have that an oncologist would miss?\" -> Olaparib (target: PARP1, PARP2, Genome integrity).\n\nStarting from a molecular state rather than a disease, the agent scanned 13799 gene-promoter methylation features against Olaparib response across cancer types, residualizing tissue and the latent methylome axes. The funnel: 40 response candidates -> 11 functionally silenced -> 10 externally replicated (n=448 cell lines).\n\nThe top opportunity is **Olaparib -> ID2** (sensitive; evidence L5).\n\nGate 2 (the known-gene prior): Inhibitor of DNA-binding 2 \u2014 a developmental/differentiation transcription factor. NOT a canonical PARP/DDR gene; no expert would prioritize it for a PARP inhibitor.\nGate 1 (the taxonomy prior): the signal is cross-indication \u2014 same direction in 9 of 14 cancer types (1 individually significant). An indication-gated search never assembles that group.\nGate 3 (multivariate control): ID2 only surfaces after residualizing tissue + the global methylation axis, and it clears a functional bar \u2014 its promoter methylation tracks its own expression DOWN (silencing_r=-0.214), so the methylation silences the gene rather than riding along as a passenger.\n\nReplication: the association holds, same direction, across GDSC, PRISM, CTRP (GDSC rho=-0.330; PRISM rho=-0.122 (p=0.0229); CTRP rho=-0.252 (p=0.0)).\n\nHonest caveats:\n  - Single-gene methylation->response is a weak, diffuse signal; the claim is directional concordance across independent screens + functional silencing, not a large effect.\n  - Cell-line results: defensible novel hypotheses (evidence ladder ~L3-L5), not clinical claims.\n  - The agent expands the hypothesis space the expert adjudicates; it removes the structural gate, it does not replace expert judgment.",
